#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119564 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/id #uuid "005f8f3f-89eb-5e99-bf0f-82b67329661a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4910" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y+3}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y-(-3)}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-3,2)$ and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 5,3,1 \\rangle.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119565 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/id #uuid "897aa930-02ca-5f79-95d0-5ee11420da97" :question/math-academy-id "e-4911" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=-2+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,0,-2)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle -2, 4, 3 \\rangle.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z-(-2)}{3} = t, \t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z+2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119566 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/id #uuid "df314d1d-56c8-5503-b632-8a425b47070f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4912" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/792bf09ff2546903b863a879a2518c42bbd30fdfce652bd4e22b1ad19aefc7c9.png)" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/bf6bd67f4c98a8d36673d51a9fd9ee42ce53c45dcd793e19b36796908be0dfca.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/80d08830e9c7790842989560740c1a8c09a2cf794399799fa44f0bfd302f165a.png) Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/54f43a22493f78d42649844a6e360d5226cc8cb7c19af11c8e8b595354063789.png) which is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/9eec5064637ab51b30b8bcae7d33babac63edd10da36315ab18c61da76f7b102.png)\n\nUsing the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/c9b75fa5f325825788ae2a64e227779c81af2454230ded498d7245dd9e0c66cf.png) and the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/dee4f9a47a1bdff7b5bee4b328768e8967ff52dba70800b6defa0575a7034847.png), we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/fdf49c280c1dcb705153d75b3f52527d7a3d6e5be88fc2497d69553ea9de953b.png)\nor equivalently\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/d606ca3e74d93f6a3e4d802603a1b27729301cd7b9611ed94d0514a6a2997f4b.png)\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/7153fc0366e9f632513e320313657591fe8f5a4238f1f0987ada184183e462ac.png) involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/d0a37c7a07a4a5e017e3edb659fad8defff4fa33a3accbd0e6fd2d6d8439cf94.png) component always maintains the constant value ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/b7f9100157159abea8f5f7a86a272d5a539e2d8084e8c894642bc49342e2a76f.png) For example, our two points ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/ad5d9528b6a85fa9d0e010a6c7b0fe7d51253b71fe995a2010204708256aa081.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/5a762bf2a30c7fb3be3ba2e1d8abf1b308614d123e11600d93d7b3b02fe828c7.png) both have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/fdc688a7d7010425aace3db801008b0b83b950e7ff7c03a89f9745d4651e1dca.png)-coordinates of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/9626663f5000cd7c8c37eeec3c498de49124f3cc6807fcaf713b85e6a692a595.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/7ff8eda495247902c9dfd90af00e4544a0090cab531689e7b7a833bce66fd9ac.png) part and instead write ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/f0d48b15791c63393dea1717a24819d4ba7502f28b54a363f5c9890b0bdb1079.png) as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/41d29b651c736cf13b6cbde92ffc46ae33bfb3e139ebec8aff2f7c05f431fde7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "74fb2af1-d791-5687-98cc-0b2db5bd8464" :question/math-academy-id "e-5021" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/fea24c391122278ffe20ec0f6a7d704b835aca18c3bdcd7395aeeea0f593a2bf.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/368f42200b9366547bdff9840a894b63d3df43722052ecb1a4e15e2306b4e56a.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b38d084-9364-5f8d-b952-f84fddc23161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316434} {:answer/id #uuid "86238f05-491a-5ea2-8d21-78f77b2eb000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316435} {:answer/id #uuid "a1107e78-407e-5977-9e7b-cf96c81c9293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316436} {:answer/id #uuid "8bef4691-51b6-549e-9d1c-7f17fd2fff85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316437} {:answer/id #uuid "978076e2-80c4-5eac-9088-3ebff7356c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1107e78-407e-5977-9e7b-cf96c81c9293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316436} :answer-field/id #uuid "793741a6-9263-5d51-9444-560559743e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef077b10-897a-59ae-a756-7a254900bdb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-127068" :question/problem "Find the vector equation of the line $\\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z+3}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{7}\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(2,1,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,-2,7⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284ff7f2-5e1e-531c-9fcd-48f864e85c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316441} {:answer/id #uuid "6242b794-79e1-5ce6-86ce-08aa9d7c23eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316442} {:answer/id #uuid "3f71be50-4818-5f61-8636-c822c1f63d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316443} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8345f1-2d6a-55df-80e4-1078245715e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316444} {:answer/id #uuid "bab9505f-1030-5316-a396-4880e36937d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bab9505f-1030-5316-a396-4880e36937d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316445} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c7e9db-9bb6-518b-b173-927099c3f520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b4f7627-0162-5a60-8c0e-f1bcbe211fae" :question/math-academy-id "q-127069" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x-4}{3}=\\frac{y-7}{-1}=\\frac{z+6}{-7}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{3}=\\frac{y-7}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-6)}{-7},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(4,7,-6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,-1,-7⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e88e358-756d-5c5d-a4cc-5ed7dcd26fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{-2}=\\frac{y-3}{5}=\\frac{z+3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316448} {:answer/id #uuid "3ace73dc-415c-501c-b51d-216f60dcbe4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316449} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fa192-4ede-5a9e-bd9f-75e6cc660f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+5}{-3}=\\frac{z-3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316450} {:answer/id #uuid "6388d026-50f4-5a7f-bbb4-0ca3efb823ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316451} {:answer/id #uuid "46f124a8-2503-535f-b345-376c65b0fa64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f124a8-2503-535f-b345-376c65b0fa64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316452} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec7fd33a-18e5-5585-9475-633a2028521a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f0384bb-f286-5414-906c-44f0eb2d0482" :question/math-academy-id "q-127397" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(-2,3,3)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨2,-5,3⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a4881ae-6bc1-5f90-b70c-043beb82b279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{1}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316455} {:answer/id #uuid "829b1d8e-8047-52cb-9898-30c4d02c732d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{3}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316456} {:answer/id #uuid "eedceb47-b18a-5aac-bb91-7894c3cad511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316457} {:answer/id #uuid "2c26ae25-d301-5afd-8f9f-03240b793bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{7}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316458} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf460ff-83f6-57b3-aa74-173cc07e0c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{7}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eedceb47-b18a-5aac-bb91-7894c3cad511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316457} :answer-field/id #uuid "1012f382-4b56-5507-aa23-c2c9fb3c03f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a52bde3-cf2a-51be-88be-91b201e6fa9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-127406" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(7,-2,4)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨1,-2,3⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7a0ebd7-374d-5121-99f6-6de8e92fcb37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{-3}=\\frac{z-1}{2},\\,y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316462} {:answer/id #uuid "c10e5aed-cf1c-5e07-8c83-9d1709baff68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316463} {:answer/id #uuid "61bf33c7-cdc9-5ec0-969c-506bda763315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z+1}{-2},\\,y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316464} {:answer/id #uuid "11250407-1bd6-53c3-934a-a11d2358c2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{z+1}{-2},\\,y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316465} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7943e9-bc79-5d8b-9d53-8f4dbe8253d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b7943e9-bc79-5d8b-9d53-8f4dbe8253d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316466} :answer-field/id #uuid "cef0f500-ef18-57ed-939c-6c4b7927683d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20898869-78be-5170-b137-d50ec6ffe709" :question/math-academy-id "q-127412" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3+3t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1-2t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65be7eab-e99a-5dd7-84c4-86d1cb996d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{z-2}{2},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316469} {:answer/id #uuid "3318adcf-7bb9-5b0b-9756-1d6ffe1555d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316470} {:answer/id #uuid "a8070fe3-83c6-5322-a070-c2eeffcc169c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{2}=\\frac{z}{7},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316471} {:answer/id #uuid "32ca8123-3590-52e8-b5f4-0371ac13228f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{z-2}{1},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316472} {:answer/id #uuid "f8db0e3b-1195-5cce-b9df-f3d39fd777fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8db0e3b-1195-5cce-b9df-f3d39fd777fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316473} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad3b3c3c-4c60-5fe9-8882-4cf3947514ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25a771d1-622d-52c6-8734-39dc2e49e8c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-127454" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=5:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3+7t \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ part and instead write $y=5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035a6874-6c4b-5495-a0cd-db32991e6815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{6}=\\frac{y-2}{3},\\,z=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316476} {:answer/id #uuid "a290a146-754d-5108-a099-f99363cd7ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{-6}=\\frac{y-2}{-3},\\,z=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316477} {:answer/id #uuid "000edebc-1e5e-52b0-a9de-f20fe0034291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-6}=\\frac{y+2}{3},\\,z=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316478} {:answer/id #uuid "40116425-bfed-5f5a-ab95-3e4695a81e95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{-6}=\\frac{y+2}{-3},\\,z=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316479} {:answer/id #uuid "e1741111-296e-512c-8f6b-f8faf63425c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{6}=\\frac{y-2}{3},\\,z=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "000edebc-1e5e-52b0-a9de-f20fe0034291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-6}=\\frac{y+2}{3},\\,z=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316478} :answer-field/id #uuid "a25ad256-2afe-542f-bd14-1da74a18de5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec5e4c78-d2dc-529b-bc3a-14014c27c99d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127535" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/44b0b683358bb448229380402717f396d13f82d2904bbf1ece1fcf9fd4567f14.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(7,-2,4)$ and $Q(1,1,4).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,1,4⟩-⟨7,-2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-6}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{3}=\\frac{z-4}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-6}=\\frac{y+2}{3}=\\frac{z-4}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\frac{z-4}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=4.$ For example, our two points $P(7,-2,4)$ and $Q(1,1,4)$ both have $z$-coordinates of $z=4.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-4}{0}$ part and instead write $z=4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-6}=\\frac{y+2}{3},\\,z=4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "512a8197-caa7-5eab-b7c7-66a1191f39a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316483} {:answer/id #uuid "95225742-41f4-5f84-8597-065439478393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y}{3}=\\frac{z}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316484} {:answer/id #uuid "3de067b2-e829-592b-81db-c3a3b6247b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316485} {:answer/id #uuid "15dcdc34-f585-5a5d-bc6d-8262b77214a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-3}{-1}=\\frac{z+1}{3},\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316486} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e251b1-26b5-5b29-b7fe-725714d987b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-3}{-1}=\\frac{z+1}{3},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "512a8197-caa7-5eab-b7c7-66a1191f39a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316483} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb47a688-ab4b-5fef-8e62-4ec8473de07c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba3c810b-166e-5700-9ed9-2bd016f2db70" :question/math-academy-id "q-32827" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(0,-1,3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,3,-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a214e0dd-c5f7-56c0-bcc3-0066251d066e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316490} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa083d4-63e8-51d2-9073-2efef9626234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=4+t \\\\ z=3-t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316491} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8eff61-2342-59ca-85df-e736f0507a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316492} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd7c90b-9b5c-5578-9d30-9ad89e8460df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316493} {:answer/id #uuid "fc35b705-6ffd-552f-91d9-e64cdac2abf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2t \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=3t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbd7c90b-9b5c-5578-9d30-9ad89e8460df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316493} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff32e8e6-b1fe-5d99-a72d-be93068c71c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "870015bf-07ab-5712-87fe-155681e8b5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-32936" :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{4}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(2,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,4,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098f0753-864a-547c-b5b4-62d2e81d199a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316496} {:answer/id #uuid "628be6c1-6537-5167-ae36-5262519a5123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316497} {:answer/id #uuid "34dff56a-5b79-5f15-b06d-9afe5e795a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316498} {:answer/id #uuid "1194370e-3a19-5f5f-a36e-23c23f28dee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316499} {:answer/id #uuid "ff787d70-728d-58a2-bc05-0482309769a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff787d70-728d-58a2-bc05-0482309769a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316500} :answer-field/id #uuid "80486b68-f79f-5ff9-800b-33882289ef97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96e088b3-5211-5361-9c40-899f5606d918" :question/math-academy-id "q-33004" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-1,0)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,2,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b860324c-156a-5ff8-91d0-403ee591eee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{2}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z-6}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316503} {:answer/id #uuid "14a0c2d7-9f78-523a-8cd3-f9f8a1e40a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y}{5}=\\frac{z}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316504} {:answer/id #uuid "f42f47f1-fe4e-5d7c-8871-6c3c385e2425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z-1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316505} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7d0308-21a1-5aaf-b13e-d45694687799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{5}=\\frac{z}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316506} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecb2359-2362-568c-9193-fba9c11b4c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ecb2359-2362-568c-9193-fba9c11b4c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316507} :answer-field/id #uuid "81118208-5437-5ad1-8c18-7d962b66c791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03ad955a-85ff-5b7d-aafe-d9e8bb434b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-33006" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+4t \\\\ y=5+5t \\\\ z=-1+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4t \\\\ 5+5t \\\\ -1+6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,5,-1)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,5,6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a03fc460-8b30-5116-95a4-a1379b48ccdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 7}{2} = \\frac{y + 4}{3}, z = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117577} {:answer/id #uuid "aedc3c5b-2001-5cee-a7cf-3d47e8dd8507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{7} = \\frac{y}{-4} = \\frac{z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117578} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dac11f-00a7-5872-8616-18cfa31e8c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 2}{7} = \\frac{y - 3}{-4} = \\frac{z - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117579} {:answer/id #uuid "e32052cf-2e0b-5211-95e6-6b7935c21229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 2}{2} = \\frac{y - 3}{3} = \\frac{z - 1}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117580} {:answer/id #uuid "74db0c86-9c4e-5972-84ec-6572f57c17cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 7}{2} = \\frac{y + 4}{3} = \\frac{z - 3}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2dac11f-00a7-5872-8616-18cfa31e8c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x + 2}{7} = \\frac{y - 3}{-4} = \\frac{z - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117579} :answer-field/id #uuid "55656267-8b79-5816-951f-12b6d1fa6ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "deb4cbaf-5918-5a60-ba36-d2bbb72dfbda" :question/math-academy-id "q-33234" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/1f9599192c1979f40fc519458019ed3296d9a5ef9048aaed41aab973c62e0be8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-2,3,1)$ and $Q(5,-1,4).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,-1,4⟩-⟨-2,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b38041ef-f5ad-5f98-835f-61500cd8f7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{y+5}{6}=\\frac{z-7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316510} {:answer/id #uuid "b0388943-ad11-526a-8826-03bfb44c15d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{y}{6}=\\frac{z}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316511} {:answer/id #uuid "acac261a-de6a-5bfb-8ca1-cc20367358c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{y+6}{-5}=\\frac{z+9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316512} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4f9bf8-d08f-5bcf-83fc-db6813ae2de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{y-6}{-5}=\\frac{z-9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316513} {:answer/id #uuid "05352976-ee1b-5b61-8232-9560a531693d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{y-5}{6}=\\frac{z+7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b38041ef-f5ad-5f98-835f-61500cd8f7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{y+5}{6}=\\frac{z-7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316510} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0055738-bf32-57e7-a545-67b369dd64b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d46d489-a3da-58f9-8831-498d49fadfa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-33249" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(3,-5,7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,6,9⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{y-(-5)}{6}=\\frac{z-7}{9}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{y+5}{6}=\\frac{z-7}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ee9e52-276f-5915-b926-0ae5ac9e5894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316517} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a3d7ac-45eb-522a-9e2a-fa2b3de00750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316518} {:answer/id #uuid "38a59d92-87f6-585c-8688-146c4c3abd2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316519} {:answer/id #uuid "d44c3e80-f458-5871-a031-d51d974d3508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316520} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe7c548-aa25-5238-a681-f7c2ef1ecd87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ee9e52-276f-5915-b926-0ae5ac9e5894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316517} :answer-field/id #uuid "65fc0554-eae8-5e9a-a146-1187aea420ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db82d7c2-73cf-50e8-96e7-cb2b24cdf2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-70832" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z+4}{3}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z-(-4)}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(3,1,-4)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,1,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64385382-c507-533a-9b07-1c09bca60793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{5}=\\frac{z+7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316524} {:answer/id #uuid "64f006f7-7110-52c2-967f-369700e3bfe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{2}=\\frac{y-5}{-3}=\\frac{z+6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316525} {:answer/id #uuid "933861c6-91ab-5956-b932-587f993220e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316526} {:answer/id #uuid "1b95e6f2-06b2-5cdc-9070-9418b85275cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{5}=\\frac{z}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316527} {:answer/id #uuid "17c61443-5940-554e-953a-e513e2167a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{2}=\\frac{y+5}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "933861c6-91ab-5956-b932-587f993220e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316526} :answer-field/id #uuid "21465bc2-9ec2-5647-9dfa-2cc98839b17f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31245656-1a21-5b83-a356-edbca20a8f50" :question/math-academy-id "q-71020" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+4t \\\\ y=-3+5t \\\\ z=7-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4t \\\\ -3+5t \\\\ 7-6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(2,-3,7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,5,-6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "997a8bb9-5471-5ecb-a7b7-320e75afdadb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{3}=\\frac{y-6}{4}=\\frac{z-9}{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316531} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee13250-3468-5dfc-8569-8e6f8e276ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{6}=\\frac{z}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316532} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe979de-e2c4-5114-9b0c-54de6bb51621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{4}=\\frac{y-4}{6}=\\frac{z+5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316533} {:answer/id #uuid "19757069-f3e5-5478-ba7c-5738429fc6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{3}=\\frac{y+6}{4}=\\frac{z+9}{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316534} {:answer/id #uuid "af86d517-949c-5ba9-a5a3-19147a5d4af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{4}=\\frac{y+4}{6}=\\frac{z-5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fe979de-e2c4-5114-9b0c-54de6bb51621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{4}=\\frac{y-4}{6}=\\frac{z+5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316533} :answer-field/id #uuid "24af8743-df28-5e94-a851-06ffdbb93235" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ec236c7-d95b-5b40-b967-e5bc6609bd1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71029" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(3,4,-5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,6,9⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{4}=\\frac{y-4}{6}=\\frac{z-(-5)}{9}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{4}=\\frac{y-4}{6}=\\frac{z+5}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e84de808-3692-524b-813b-321d10c0a09b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{9}=\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316538} {:answer/id #uuid "99d6eebf-7242-5454-8408-a833ab3b3442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+9}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{-5}=\\frac{z-7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316539} {:answer/id #uuid "4a920040-c93b-5d7a-bab2-67007d717d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316540} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3a56cf-9179-5758-801c-2898b6f9fab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{9}=\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z+3}{-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316541} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb75d30-8f29-58ea-b094-f7b263390113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fb75d30-8f29-58ea-b094-f7b263390113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316542} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0e5313-74b4-5137-862e-04fc9543ef40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67dee0f1-a305-5544-8b63-7dcdcefbb7bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-71030" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(9,3,-7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨4,-5,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1e755ad-e39b-5f0c-bb44-a4c932c7bfb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-2}{-9},\\,z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316545} {:answer/id #uuid "a6251438-70a2-5914-be4d-bab80d66ba4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2},\\,z=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316546} {:answer/id #uuid "a590e034-4000-5b10-b34c-ec1b956e4c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=z-11,\\,y=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316547} {:answer/id #uuid "5deab0e9-6dbe-514b-bafb-bd27d1a64cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{-3}=\\frac{y-9}{2},\\,z=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316548} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ce48b2-2e7f-5dad-9ecc-1e06b048aec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2}=z-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6251438-70a2-5914-be4d-bab80d66ba4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2},\\,z=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316546} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fa2c10a-daa5-504b-a943-5f0ca58af690" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12484b2b-517e-53da-82a8-68ef46a1747e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71039" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y-(-9)}{2}=\\frac{z-11}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2}=\\frac{z-11}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-11}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=11:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7-3t \\\\ -9+2t \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-11}{0}$ part and instead write $z=11$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2},\\,z=11\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d1c67bc-934c-5683-a01b-521f263f411e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=\\frac{y-5}{-2}=z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316552} {:answer/id #uuid "d91c35e4-85f6-50c5-8c0c-4291a5d35c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y+2}{5},\\,z=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316553} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e6832e-a237-5195-9572-b03c4c67c919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=z,\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316554} {:answer/id #uuid "648dd039-a858-5c69-a71e-f821d1686183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-5}{-2}=z,\\,x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316555} {:answer/id #uuid "c85bc298-fef8-545e-b666-409ba57aa9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+5}{-2}=z,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "648dd039-a858-5c69-a71e-f821d1686183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-5}{-2}=z,\\,x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316555} :answer-field/id #uuid "80ef1a4e-9e9b-55a8-9c9e-de406d172759" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a54b426-ecf5-557b-8288-a133688ba386" :question/math-academy-id "q-71045" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{y-5}{-2}=\\frac{z-0}{1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{0}=\\frac{y-5}{-2}=z,\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5-2t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-5}{-2}=z,\\,x=-3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdf4a3a1-30a5-5dba-8526-3eb2a68d2150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3},\\,z=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316559} {:answer/id #uuid "f150c224-2fb0-5d8f-b8b0-53363c150bca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-1}{-3},\\,z=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316560} {:answer/id #uuid "51bbf9d7-ebb6-57d9-9db1-377f308a7ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-7}{3}=\\frac{y+3}{-1},\\,z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316561} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e9c5bb-e8c2-5b1e-80ba-2229ed350480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+7}{3}=\\frac{y-3}{-1},\\,z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316562} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5b386d-4e12-559d-add5-c299c2ec74f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3}=z-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdf4a3a1-30a5-5dba-8526-3eb2a68d2150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3},\\,z=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316559} :answer-field/id #uuid "bba3a63e-a2b8-5a00-8c7b-3dad51c6269f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa1fb4ff-b4b1-5558-a7f2-a6d8fc0b693f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71048" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-6}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=6:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3+7t \\\\ -1-3t \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-6}{0}$ part and instead write $z=6$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3},\\,z=6\n$$"}]}
